Crawled: last month; Description: Interviews with thirty-two people, Chinese and European, who lived and/or worked in the Wellington Chinatown area around Haining Street up to the 1960s.\n\n# Haining Street (Wellington, N.Z.) - History\n\nFavourite [Input]\n\nThere are 1 related items to this topic\n\nSee all related items\n\nImage\n\n### Haining Street Oral History Project\n\nDate: 1994 - 2004\n\nBy: Shum, Lynette Pauline, 1957-\n\nReference: OHColl-0455\n\nDescription: Interviews with thirty-two people, Chinese and European, who lived and/or worked in the Wellington Chinatown area around Haining Street up to the 1960s. Those born in China talk about their family histories as well as growing up in the area. Others who worked in the area include two former police officers. Interviewees are Sunly Chong, Sydney Choy, Wellington Choy, Doris Chung, Morris DeTerte, Ken Douglas, Nancy Goddard, Jim Gow, Winnifred Hoy, Joyce Jarrold, Ron Leng, Maureen Leong, Jessie Lo, Joy Matthews, Elspeth Jean Munro, Allan Ng, David Paterson, Sam Shum, Bob Silk, Alan Thomas, Hong Tse, Vince Walter, Jack Weeds, Bing Wong, Charles Wong, Harry Wong, Ray Wong, William Wong, William Wong Hop, Ian Yee, Martin Yee, Raymond Young. Six photographs donated by Bob Silk are relevant to the entire collection. Interviewer(s) - Lynette Shum Quantity: 33 interview(s). 91 C60 cassette(s). 2 digital sound recording(s) digital sound recording(s). 25 printed abstract(s). 7 folder(s) biographical information forms and printed epheemra. 6 digital photograph(s). Search dates: 1994 - 2004 Number of interviews/events: 33 Processing information: Some interviews have only brief records No abstracts for Sydney Choy, Winifred Hoy, Maureen Leong, Hong Tse, Bing Wong, Ian Yee, Martin Yee.\n\nView on tiaki\n\nSee all related items--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHeritage Partners (https://www.nineyearstheatre.com/Documents/E-programmes/Home%20Kitchen_EN.pdf)\nciteturn8search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; Pek Sin Choon supplies tea to over 80 percent of the Bak Kut Teh market segment, making tea an integral component of this unique culinary culture.\nHeritage Partners\n\nPek Sin Choon\n\nPek Sin Choon Pte Ltd is a Singapore-based tea merchant located along Mosque Street in the Chinatown heritage district.\n\nFounded in 1925, the near century-old company has since established itself as the foremost purveyor of premium Chinese tea in the city-state. Pek Sin Choon supplies tea to over 80 percent of the Bak Kut Teh market segment, making tea an integral component of this unique culinary culture.\n\nToday, the company is helmed by fourth generation descendent and tea man Peh Ching Her, better known as Kenry Peh. The company continues to source, blend and roast tea the traditional way. Each pack of tea is painstakingly packed by hand in-store.\n\nWebsite: https://peksinchoon.com  \nInstagram: @peksinchoon  \nFacebook: @PSC1925\n\nHear stories from third generation tea merchant, Mdm Lim Boh Tun and the current owner, fourth generation tea merchant, Mr Kenry Peh, on both Pek Sin Choon & Nine Years Theatre Facebook and Instagram accounts!\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nLogo image for Pek Sin Choon: red Chinese characters and the words “Pek Sin Choon” above a black buffalo silhouette with a seated figure.\n</visual_element>\n\n<visual_element id=\"e2\">\nLarge photo of a tea shop display cabinet filled with rows of colorful tea boxes and gold tea packages behind glass doors with orange Chinese lettering.\n</visual_element>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNational Heritage Board (https://www.nhb.gov.sg/-/media/nhb/files/media/releases/2025/media-release---celebrating-heritage-practitioners-and-nurturing-youth-stewards-at-the-stewards-of-ich-award-2024.pdf)\nciteturn8search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; <td><strong>Attributed to: Mr Kenry Peh, fourth-generation owne